I do not see the draft or schedule in the conversation available to me. **Please paste the 3-week schedule and state the cleanroom grade/class and whether the line is GMP-regulated.**

Provisional assessment: **3 weeks would often be too short if the optical system requires formal change control and IQ/OQ/PQ before production use.** It may be feasible for a non-production trial using an approved temporary-change process.

Check whether the schedule explicitly includes:

- change-control initiation, impact/risk assessment, and approval;
- contamination-control review, cleanroom entry approval, and equipment/material cleaning;
- facilities, electrical, network, and environmental-monitoring impacts;
- calibration and measurement-system analysis;
- installation qualification and operational qualification;
- performance qualification across representative products, defects, shifts, and operators;
- software or computerized-system validation, data integrity, access controls, and audit trails where applicable;
- SOP/work-instruction updates and operator training;
- deviation resolution, report approval, and quality release.

The likely constraint is not physical installation but **protocol approval, validation execution, exception closure, and Quality approval**. For capital review, label the 3 weeks as an **installation and controlled pilot window**, not a validated production-release commitment, unless Quality and Validation have confirmed those lead times in writing. My recommendation is to separate the plan into: **(1) installation/pilot, (2) validation, and (3) production release**, with approval gates and named owners.

That changes the assessment. **If CR-8812 and the approved IQ/OQ records explicitly cover Line B, this hardware class, and the planned cleanroom intervention, a 3-week fixture-adaptation and calibration schedule would not typically conflict with a separate 6-week requalification lead time.**

Before capital review, verify that the records establish:

- the installed vision frame is within the pre-qualified design envelope;
- fixture changes do not alter cleanroom classification, airflow, utilities, line clearance, or validated operating parameters;
- camera, lighting, software, firmware, recipes, and data handling remain within the approved configuration;
- calibration and any required measurement-system analysis fit within the 3 weeks;
- CR-8812 identifies the required acceptance testing and Quality release gate;
- no product-specific PQ, engineering-run minimum, or post-installation environmental monitoring is still required.

A precise presentation statement would be:

> The proposed 3-week window covers fixture adaptation, calibration, and acceptance activities. Line B was previously qualified for this vision-hardware class during the November tooling overhaul. Approved change control CR-8812 and the associated IQ/OQ records govern the installation; therefore, the separate 6-week platform requalification window is not included. Production release remains subject to completion of the acceptance criteria and Quality approval specified in CR-8812.

**Recommendation:** retain the 3-week schedule, cite the exact CR-8812 and IQ/OQ sections supporting the exemption, and show Quality release as the final schedule gate.
